**Key idea:** the value predicted from cycle *n*'s inputs appears on `acc_out` in cycle *n+1*, so predictions go into a **queue** and the oldest one is compared with the current output, i.e. a delay line of depth = latency.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",          "wave": "p...." },
  { "name": "a, b, acc_in", "wave": "====x", "data": ["2,3,1", "4,5,0", "-1,6,10", "0,0,0"] },
  { "name": "acc_out (DUT)", "wave": "x====", "data": ["7", "20", "4", "0"] },
  { "name": "predicted",    "wave": "====x", "data": ["7", "20", "4", "0"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "prediction from cycle n is checked against acc_out in cycle n+1" } }
```

| Cycle | inputs (a, b, acc_in) | push | queue after push | compare |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 2, 3, 1 | 7 | [7] | size 1, nothing to compare |
| 1 | 4, 5, 0 | 20 | [7, 20] | pop 7 vs acc_out 7: match |
| 2 | -1, 6, 10 | 4 | [20, 4] | pop 20 vs acc_out 20: match |
| 3 | 0, 0, 0 | 0 | [4, 0] | pop 4 vs acc_out 4: match |

### Solution
- **Prediction in `write()`**: compute `a*b + acc_in` with the operands widened to 16 bits *before* multiplying (signedness passes through a size cast, so `-1` stays `-1`).
- **Alignment**: push, then pop only when `size() > LATENCY`. A single variable can't hold the prediction, the next cycle's input overwrites it before use.
- **Compare with `!==`**: with `!=`, an X on `acc_out` makes the condition X and the `if` silently does nothing.
- **Black-box**: everything comes through the analysis port; no hierarchical peeks into the DUT, so the checker works unchanged for another MAC.

### Code
```systemverilog
class mac_txn extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(mac_txn)
  rand bit signed [7:0]  a, b;
  rand bit signed [15:0] acc_in;
  bit signed [15:0]      acc_out;     // observed, NOT rand
  function new(string name = "mac_txn"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class mac_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard;
  `uvm_component_utils(mac_scoreboard)

  localparam int LATENCY = 1;
  uvm_analysis_imp #(mac_txn, mac_scoreboard) imp;
  bit signed [15:0] exp_q[$];
  int unsigned n_ok, n_err;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    imp = new("imp", this);
  endfunction

  function void write(mac_txn tr);
    bit signed [15:0] prod, exp_v;
    prod = 16'(tr.a) * 16'(tr.b);          // widen first, then multiply
    exp_q.push_back(prod + tr.acc_in);
    if (exp_q.size() > LATENCY) begin
      exp_v = exp_q.pop_front();
      if (tr.acc_out !== exp_v) begin
        n_err++;
        `uvm_error("MAC_SB", $sformatf("exp=%0d got=%0d", exp_v, tr.acc_out))
      end
      else n_ok++;
    end
  endfunction

  function void report_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    `uvm_info("MAC_SB", $sformatf("%0d matches, %0d mismatches", n_ok, n_err), UVM_LOW)
  endfunction
endclass

// env connect_phase:  mon.ap.connect(sb.imp);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- 8-bit x 8-bit in an 8-bit context truncates the product; the size cast fixes it.
- Reset: flush `exp_q` when reset is seen, or the first post-reset compare uses stale predictions.
- `ref` is a keyword: don't use it as a variable name.
- A latency of 2 means pop when `size() > 2`; just change `LATENCY`.

### Follow-ups
- **What if the latency varies?** A fixed-depth queue only works for fixed latency; tag transactions with an ID and match by ID (associative array of queues).
- **Why `!==` instead of `!=`?** If either side is X, `!=` is X and the error branch never runs.
- **What goes wrong if `acc_out` is `rand`?** The scoreboard compares against a random number instead of the pin value.

**Key idea:** one interface, two views. The driver's clocking block *outputs* `valid/data` slightly after the edge; the monitor's clocking block only *inputs* them, sampled just before the edge. Modports bind each view so a component can't use the wrong one.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",                         "wave": "p...." },
  { "name": "valid (cb_drv output #1)",    "wave": "0.1.0" },
  { "name": "data[0..N-1]",                "wave": "x.==x", "data": ["D0", "D1"] },
  { "name": "cb_mon.valid (input #1step)", "wave": "0..1." }
],
  "head": { "text": "driver changes after edge 2; DUT and monitor see the new value at edge 3" } }
```

| View | Clocking block | Directions | Used by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `drv` modport | `cb_drv` | `output valid, data` | driver: `vif.cb_drv.valid <= 1;` |
| `mon` modport | `cb_mon` | `input valid, data` | monitor: `@(vif.cb_mon); x = vif.cb_mon.data[i];` |
| both | n/a | `input clk, rst_n` | reset/clock waits |

### Solution
- `N` only changes the instantiation (`stream_if #(.N(8)) if0(clk, rst_n);`); driver and monitor code are unchanged if they are parameterized the same way.
- `clk` and `rst_n` are ports driven by the TB top; don't redeclare same-named signals inside.
- A modport creates no signals, it only restricts access and direction.

### Code
```systemverilog
interface stream_if #(parameter int N = 4) (
  input logic clk,
  input logic rst_n
);
  logic              valid;
  logic signed [7:0] data [N];          // N independent signed bytes

  // Driver view: drive 1 time unit after the edge (no race with the DUT)
  clocking cb_drv @(posedge clk);
    default input #1step output #1;
    output valid, data;
  endclocking

  // Monitor view: sample the value that was stable just before the edge
  clocking cb_mon @(posedge clk);
    default input #1step;
    input valid, data;
  endclocking

  modport drv (clocking cb_drv, input clk, rst_n);
  modport mon (clocking cb_mon, input clk, rst_n);
endinterface

// Driver usage
class stream_driver #(int N = 4) extends uvm_driver #(stream_item #(N));
  virtual stream_if #(N).drv vif;
  // ...
  task drive(stream_item #(N) it);
    @(vif.cb_drv);
    vif.cb_drv.valid <= 1'b1;
    foreach (it.data[i]) vif.cb_drv.data[i] <= it.data[i];
    @(vif.cb_drv);
    vif.cb_drv.valid <= 1'b0;
  endtask
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `logic signed bit [7:0]` is a syntax error: `logic` and `bit` are both data types.
- Driving `vif.valid` directly (not through `cb_drv`) bypasses the skew and reintroduces races.
- Two pin groups sharing one interface instance overwrite each other: instantiate one interface per pin group.

### Follow-ups
- **Why not output skew `#0`?** The TB would update `data` on the same edge the DUT samples it: a race.
- **Directions of `clk`/`rst_n` in each modport?** Input for both.
- **Can config_db pass a `#(4)` handle to a component expecting `#(8)`?** No: different specializations are different types; the `get` fails.

**Key idea:** the virtual sequencer is just a *container of handles* to real sequencers. The virtual sequence runs on it, reaches the real sequencers through `p_sequencer`, and starts each child on its own sequencer inside `fork ... join`.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  TEST["test run_phase: raise objection"] -->|start on env.vseqr| VSEQ["layered_sequence on virtual_sequencer"]
  VSEQ -->|fork| CSEQ["cmd_sequence"]
  VSEQ -->|fork| DSEQ["data_sequence"]
  CSEQ -->|start on cmd_seqr| CSQR["cmd_seqr in cmd_agent"]
  DSEQ -->|start on data_seqr| DSQR["data_seqr in data_agent"]
  CSQR --> CDRV["cmd_driver"]
  DSQR --> DDRV["data_driver"]
  CDRV --> JOIN["join: both finished"]
  DDRV --> JOIN
  JOIN --> DROP["drop objection"]
```

### Solution
- **Virtual sequencer**: two handle fields, no `create` of them.
- **Env connect_phase**: `vseqr.cmd_seqr = cmd_agt.seqr; vseqr.data_seqr = data_agt.seqr;` (the agents create the real sequencers in build_phase, so connect_phase is the earliest safe point).
- **Layered sequence**: `` `uvm_declare_p_sequencer(virtual_sequencer) `` casts `m_sequencer` to the right type when the sequence starts.
- **Test**: objection around `seq.start(env.vseqr)`, otherwise the phase ends immediately and kills both streams.

### Code
```systemverilog
class virtual_sequencer extends uvm_sequencer;
  `uvm_component_utils(virtual_sequencer)
  cmd_sequencer  cmd_seqr;     // handles only, assigned by the env
  data_sequencer data_seqr;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction
endclass

class layered_sequence extends uvm_sequence;
  `uvm_object_utils(layered_sequence)
  `uvm_declare_p_sequencer(virtual_sequencer)

  function new(string name = "layered_sequence"); super.new(name); endfunction

  task body();
    cmd_sequence  cseq = cmd_sequence::type_id::create("cseq");
    data_sequence dseq = data_sequence::type_id::create("dseq");
    fork
      cseq.start(p_sequencer.cmd_seqr);
      dseq.start(p_sequencer.data_seqr);
    join                       // wait for BOTH
  endtask
endclass

// env
function void my_env::connect_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  vseqr.cmd_seqr  = cmd_agt.seqr;
  vseqr.data_seqr = data_agt.seqr;
endfunction

// test
task my_test::run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  layered_sequence seq = layered_sequence::type_id::create("seq");
  phase.raise_objection(this);
  seq.start(env.vseqr);
  phase.drop_objection(this);
endtask
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Handles assigned in build_phase may still be null (agent not built yet): use connect_phase.
- Declaring your own `p_sequencer` variable and assigning it by hand is not equivalent to the macro.
- If the protocol requires each command to precede its data, drop the `fork` and start them sequentially.

### Follow-ups
- **Why not start the layered sequence on `cmd_seqr`?** That sequencer doesn't hold the two handles; the `p_sequencer` cast fails.
- **What must each child's `start` receive?** Its own lower-level sequencer.
- **Without the objection?** Both streams stop partway through.
- **Command must lead data by some margin?** Keep the `fork` but make the data branch wait on an event/flag the command side triggers; don't use `join_none` and don't count clocks inside a sequence.

**Key idea:** bound `burst_len` globally, then narrow WRITE with an implication. In the cross, the WRITE x {2,3,5,6,7} cells are *unreachable by design*, so exclude them (`ignore_bins`) instead of loosening the constraint, and keep an `illegal_bins` check to catch anything that bypasses it.

| access \ burst_len | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| READ | hit | hit | hit | hit | hit | hit | hit | hit |
| WRITE | hit | ignored | ignored | hit | ignored | ignored | ignored | hit |

8 + 3 = **11 reachable cross bins**; with the `ignore_bins` the cross can reach 100%.

### Solution
- `burst_len inside {[1:8]}` is required: the implication says nothing about READ, and `int` would happily randomize to `-5`.
- Embedded covergroup: constructed in `new()`, sampled explicitly.
- `cp_wr` with `iff (access_type == WRITE)` holds the `illegal_bins` for illegal WRITE lengths.

### Code
```systemverilog
typedef enum {READ, WRITE} access_e;

class mem_txn extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(mem_txn)

  rand bit [7:0] addr;
  rand access_e  access_type;
  rand int       burst_len;

  constraint c_burst {
    burst_len inside {[1:8]};                              // all accesses
    (access_type == WRITE) -> burst_len inside {1, 4, 8};  // writes only
  }

  covergroup cg;
    cp_access: coverpoint access_type;
    cp_burst:  coverpoint burst_len { bins len[] = {[1:8]}; }
    cp_addr:   coverpoint addr { bins lo = {[0:127]}; bins hi = {[128:255]}; }
    cx:        cross cp_access, cp_burst {
      ignore_bins wr_unreachable = binsof(cp_access) intersect {WRITE} &&
                                   binsof(cp_burst)  intersect {[2:3], [5:7]};
    }
    cp_wr:     coverpoint burst_len iff (access_type == WRITE) {
      bins         legal[] = {1, 4, 8};
      illegal_bins bad     = {[2:3], [5:7]};
    }
  endgroup

  function new(string name = "mem_txn");
    super.new(name);
    cg = new();
  endfunction

  function void sample();
    cg.sample();
  endfunction
endclass

// In the sequence:
//   start_item(tr);
//   if (!tr.randomize()) `uvm_fatal("RND", "randomize failed")
//   tr.sample();            // after randomize, before the driver modifies it
//   finish_item(tr);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Sampling too early sees the previous transaction; too late and the driver may already have changed fields. Many teams sample in the **monitor** instead, which covers what actually hit the bus.
- `bins all[] = {[0:255]}` creates 256 bins: wasteful unless every address matters.
- Don't convert `illegal_bins` to `ignore_bins` just to reach 100%: that hides a real constraint bug.

### Follow-ups
- **`illegal_bins` vs `ignore_bins`?** Illegal raises an error when hit; ignore removes values from coverage (and from the denominator).
- **Some cross cells can never be hit?** Exclude them with `ignore_bins` (or `binsof ... intersect`), never by loosening the constraint.
- **Distribution:** to favor long reads, add `burst_len dist {[1:4] := 1, [5:8] := 3};` under `access_type == READ`.

**Key idea:** the agent class has no idea which port it owns. The **tb top** publishes each interface under an absolute path, the **env** publishes `is_active` per instance, and the agent `get`s both with `get(this, "", ...)` and builds a driver/sequencer only when active.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  TB["tb_top: if0, if1"] -->|set with null ctx and abs path| CDB["uvm_config_db"]
  ENV["env build_phase"] -->|set is_active per agent| CDB
  CDB -->|drv_vif + mon_vif + ACTIVE| A0["agent0"]
  CDB -->|mon_vif + PASSIVE| A1["agent1"]
  A0 --> D0["driver + sequencer"]
  A0 --> M0["monitor"]
  A1 --> M1["monitor only"]
  D0 -->|drives| IF0["if0 pins"]
  M0 -->|samples| IF0
  M1 -->|samples| IF1["if1 pins"]
```

| `set` called from | context arg | path arg | resolves to |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| tb top `initial` | `null` | `"uvm_test_top.env.agent0"` | `uvm_test_top.env.agent0` |
| test | `this` | `"env.agent0"` | `uvm_test_top.env.agent0` |
| env | `this` | `"agent0"` | `uvm_test_top.env.agent0` |

### Code
```systemverilog
// tb top: interfaces are instantiated here
module tb_top;
  logic clk, rst_n;
  stream_if #(4) if0 (clk, rst_n);
  stream_if #(4) if1 (clk, rst_n);
  initial begin
    uvm_config_db#(virtual stream_if#(4).drv)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agent0", "drv_vif", if0);
    uvm_config_db#(virtual stream_if#(4).mon)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agent0", "mon_vif", if0);
    uvm_config_db#(virtual stream_if#(4).mon)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agent1", "mon_vif", if1);
    run_test();
  end
endmodule

// env build_phase
function void my_env::build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  uvm_config_db#(uvm_active_passive_enum)::set(this, "agent0", "is_active", UVM_ACTIVE);
  uvm_config_db#(uvm_active_passive_enum)::set(this, "agent1", "is_active", UVM_PASSIVE);
  agent0 = stream_agent::type_id::create("agent0", this);
  agent1 = stream_agent::type_id::create("agent1", this);
endfunction

// The agent, written once
class stream_agent extends uvm_agent;
  `uvm_component_utils(stream_agent)
  virtual stream_if#(4).drv vif_drv;
  virtual stream_if#(4).mon vif_mon;
  stream_driver    drv;
  stream_sequencer seqr;
  stream_monitor   mon;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction

  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(uvm_active_passive_enum)::get(this, "", "is_active", is_active))
      `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "is_active not set")
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual stream_if#(4).mon)::get(this, "", "mon_vif", vif_mon))
      `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "mon_vif not set")
    mon = stream_monitor::type_id::create("mon", this);

    if (is_active == UVM_ACTIVE) begin
      if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual stream_if#(4).drv)::get(this, "", "drv_vif", vif_drv))
        `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "drv_vif not set")
      drv  = stream_driver::type_id::create("drv", this);
      seqr = stream_sequencer::type_id::create("seqr", this);
    end
  endfunction

  function void connect_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    mon.vif = vif_mon;
    if (is_active == UVM_ACTIVE) begin
      drv.vif = vif_drv;
      drv.seq_item_port.connect(seqr.seq_item_export);
    end
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- One path segment too many or too few, or a misspelled field name, makes `get` return 0 and the vif stays null: always check the return value.
- `virtual stream_if#(4)` and `virtual stream_if#(4).mon` are different types for config_db.
- If `is_active` is never set it defaults to `UVM_ACTIVE`: a port meant to be passive gets driven.

### Follow-ups
- **Setting from the tb `initial`?** No `this` there: pass `null` and an absolute path.
- **Moving a `set` from the test into the env?** The relative path changes from `env.agent0` to `agent0`.
- **Debugging a failed `get`?** `+UVM_CONFIG_DB_TRACE`, or `uvm_config_db_options::turn_on_tracing()`.

**Key idea:** the middle step is a **poll loop of real bus reads**, not a fixed delay. Each read's data is only valid after `finish_item` returns, so the check happens after the transfer, in a bounded `do ... while`.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant SEQ as ctrl_status_seq
  participant SQR as sequencer
  participant DRV as driver
  SEQ->>SQR: start_item(ctrl_wr)
  SQR-->>SEQ: grant
  SEQ->>DRV: finish_item, write 0x0 = ctrl_value
  DRV-->>SEQ: item_done
  loop until STATUS bit0 == 1, at most 100 reads
    SEQ->>DRV: read 0x4
    DRV-->>SEQ: item_done, req.data = STATUS
  end
  SEQ->>DRV: write 0x8 = 0xDEADBEEF
  DRV-->>SEQ: item_done
```

### Solution
- `start_item` blocks until the sequencer grants; randomizing *after* the grant lets late constraints (e.g. current state) apply.
- `finish_item` returns when the driver calls `item_done()`; for a read the driver has copied the bus data into `req.data` by then.
- New object per transfer: reusing a completed item risks carrying the previous address/data into the next one.

### Code
```systemverilog
class ctrl_status_seq extends uvm_sequence #(reg_seq_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(ctrl_status_seq)

  rand bit [31:0] ctrl_value;          // set by the test before start()
  int             max_polls = 100;

  function new(string name = "ctrl_status_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction

  task body();
    reg_seq_item req;
    bit [31:0]   status;
    int          polls = 0;

    // 1. Write CTRL
    req = reg_seq_item::type_id::create("ctrl_wr");
    start_item(req);
    if (!req.randomize() with { addr == 32'h0; data == ctrl_value; read_write == 0; })
      `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "randomize failed")
    finish_item(req);

    // 2. Poll STATUS bit 0, bounded
    do begin
      req = reg_seq_item::type_id::create("st_rd");
      start_item(req);
      if (!req.randomize() with { addr == 32'h4; read_write == 1; })
        `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "randomize failed")
      finish_item(req);
      status = req.data;               // valid only after finish_item
      polls++;
      if (!status[0] && polls >= max_polls) begin
        `uvm_error(get_full_name(), $sformatf("STATUS not ready after %0d polls", polls))
        return;                        // the test decides how to end
      end
    end while (!status[0]);

    // 3. Write the datapath register
    req = reg_seq_item::type_id::create("dp_wr");
    start_item(req);
    if (!req.randomize() with { addr == 32'h8; data == 32'hDEAD_BEEF; read_write == 0; })
      `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "randomize failed")
    finish_item(req);
  endtask
endclass

