```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Checking `polls >= 100` without `!status[0]` reports a timeout even when the 100th read succeeded.
- If the driver uses a separate response (`put_response`), call `get_response(rsp)` and read `rsp.data` instead of `req.data`.
- With a RAL model the same flow is `regmodel.CTRL.write()`, a `STATUS.read()` loop (or `mirror`), then a datapath write.

### Follow-ups
- **What if `randomize()` fails?** Stop with `uvm_fatal`; never `finish_item` an unrandomized item.
- **Why not `@(posedge clk)` in the sequence?** A sequence usually has no vif, and clock counting doesn't line up with bus timing: just issue another read.
- **Who ends the sim after a timeout?** The test, via its objection.

**Key idea:** two instances of the *same* config sequence (different `base_addr`) run on two sequencers inside `fork ... join`; the datapath sequence is the next statement after `join`, so it can't start until the slower block finishes.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",             "wave": "p............." },
  { "name": "bus A (seqr_a)",  "wave": "x=====xxxxxxxx", "data": ["A0", "A1", "A2", "A3", "A4"] },
  { "name": "bus B (seqr_b)",  "wave": "x=.=.=.=.=.xxx", "data": ["B0", "B1", "B2", "B3", "B4"] },
  { "name": "datapath (join)", "wave": "xxxxxxxxxxx===", "data": ["D0", "D1", "D2"] },
  {},
  { "name": "datapath if join_any (BUG)", "wave": "xxxxxx===xxxxx", "data": ["D0", "D1", "D2"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "join waits for the slower block B; join_any starts traffic while B is still configuring" } }
